Now I need to understand the vulnerability better. Let me look at the `Tlv::Get` function again:

```cpp
otError Tlv::Get(const Message &aMessage, uint8_t aType, uint16_t aMaxLength, Tlv &aTlv)
{
    otError  error = OT_ERROR_NOT_FOUND;
    uint16_t offset;

    SuccessOrExit(error = GetOffset(aMessage, aType, offset));
    aMessage.Read(offset, sizeof(Tlv), &aTlv);  // Read 2 bytes (type + length)

    if (aMaxLength > sizeof(aTlv) + aTlv.GetLength())
    {
        aMaxLength = sizeof(aTlv) + aTlv.GetLength();
    }

    aMessage.Read(offset, aMaxLength, &aTlv);  // Read up to aMaxLength bytes

exit:
    return error;
}
```

When called with `Tlv::GetTlv(aMessage, Tlv::kResponse, sizeof(response), response)`:
- `aMaxLength = sizeof(response)` = `sizeof(ResponseTlv)` = 10
- `sizeof(aTlv)` = `sizeof(Tlv)` = 2
- If `aTlv.GetLength()` = 8, then `aMaxLength = min(10, 2 + 8) = 10`
- So it reads 10 bytes from the message into the `response` variable

But wait, the `ResponseTlv` is packed and has `mResponse[8]`, so `sizeof(ResponseTlv)` = 10. Reading 10 bytes into it is fine.

